Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling
Imagine your Spring Boot service starts receiving more traffic.

                 More Traffic
                     |
               Load increases
                     |
          +----------+----------+
          |                     |
     Vertical Scaling      Horizontal Scaling
       "Scale Up"            "Scale Out"
          |                     |
     Bigger machine        More machines/pods

1. Vertical Scaling — Scale Up
You make the existing server more powerful.

Before
Spring Boot
   |
+--------+
| 4 CPU  |
| 8 GB   |
+--------+
After
Spring Boot
   |
+---------+
| 16 CPU  |
| 64 GB   |
+---------+
For example, suppose your Java service has:

4 CPU
8 GB RAM
and starts struggling.
You upgrade it to:

16 CPU
64 GB RAM
You still have one application instance, but it's much stronger.
Advantages
	• Simple architecture 
	• No distributed-system complexity 
	• No load balancer required 
	• Easier debugging 
	• Useful for databases and legacy systems 
	• Minimal application changes 
	
Trade-offs
The biggest problem is that hardware has a ceiling.

8 GB → 16 GB → 32 GB → 64 GB → 128 GB → ???

Eventually you cannot keep scaling. It can also leave you with a major single point of failure.

Users
  |
  v
Huge Server
  |
  X  server crashes
  |
Entire application unavailable And large machines can become disproportionately expensive.

2. Horizontal Scaling — Scale Out
Instead of making one server bigger, you add more instances.

                   Users
                     |
               Load Balancer
                     |
        +------------+------------+
        |                 |                |
        v                 v                 v
     Pod-1         Pod-2         Pod-3
 Spring Boot   Spring Boot   Spring Boot

For Kubernetes/OpenShift, this is exactly what you're commonly doing when increasing replicas:

replicas: 3
Becoming replicas: 10

Now traffic gets distributed across 10 Spring Boot instances.

Advantages
Horizontal scaling gives you:
	• High availability 
	• Fault tolerance 
	• Elastic scaling 
	• Much larger scalability ceiling 
	• Rolling deployments 
	• Better resilience to machine failure 
	
If one instance dies:

               Load Balancer
                /    |    \
              /      X      \
            v                  v
           Pod-1        Pod-3
the other instances can continue serving traffic. This is one reason cloud-native architectures strongly favour horizontal scaling.

The Important HLD Trade-offs
This is where I'd expect a senior engineer to go beyond saying "vertical = bigger machine, horizontal = more machines."
Area	Vertical	Horizontal
Approach	Bigger machine	More machines
Complexity	Low	Higher
Scalability	Limited	Very high
Availability	Lower	High
Fault tolerance	Poorer	Better
Load balancer	Usually unnecessary	Usually required
Distributed-system issues	Few	Many
Cost model	Powerful machines become expensive	Commodity instances
Cloud/Kubernetes	Less ideal	Excellent
Stateful apps	Easier	Harder
Operational complexity	Lower	Higher

The hidden cost of horizontal scaling
Horizontal scaling sounds superior, but it introduces distributed-system problems. Suppose you have:

               Load Balancer
              /      |      \
             /       |       \
          Pod A    Pod B    Pod C

Request 1 might hit Pod A.
Request 2 from the same user might hit Pod C.
If Pod A stored this in memory: session.setAttribute("cart", cart);
Pod C doesn't know about it. That's why horizontally scalable applications should generally aim to be stateless.

Instead:

Pod A ----\
Pod B -----+----> Redis / Database
Pod C ----/
Shared state moves outside the application instances. This affects many architectural decisions:

Sessions        → Redis / external session store
Files           → S3 / object storage
Cache           → Distributed cache
Database        → Shared/distributed database
Events          → Kafka
Configuration   → Centralized configuration

Java-specific consideration: JVM
Vertical scaling also has an interesting JVM implication. Giving a Java application:

256 GB RAM isn't automatically better than running several JVMs with smaller heaps.
Very large heaps can make GC behaviour, tuning and failure blast radius more challenging.
You might prefer:

             Load Balancer
                        |
       +-----------+-----------+
       |                |               |
     JVM-1       JVM-2       JVM-3
     8 GB          8 GB            8 GB

instead of:  JVM 24 GB depending on workload, collector, latency requirements and infrastructure.
Horizontal scaling also lets Kubernetes replace unhealthy JVM instances independently.

